Example programs and the texts they cite (examples/)

- examples/migration/*/sources/ — other systems' programs, copied so that
  their translations into Logical English (the "twins" beside them) can be
  checked against them. Each keeps its authors' copyright and licence,
  stated in a LICENSE or NOTICE file in its folder, or in the folder's
  README.md or MAKING_OF.md. A twin is a translation of its source, and so
  the licence of the source applies to the twin as well:
  - blawx/: example projects of Blawx version 1, copyright (c) 2022
    Lexpedite Legal Technologies Ltd.; MIT licence (BLAWX_LICENSE beside
    each project).
  - catala/: Catala programs from catala-examples
    (https://github.com/CatalaLang/catala-examples), by the Catala team
    (Inria) and its contributors; Apache License 2.0; copied unchanged
    (catala/README.md).
  - dmn/tck/: models of the DMN Technology Compatibility Kit (dmn-tck/tck),
    copyright the DMN TCK contributors; Apache License 2.0.
  - dmn/ronl/: example models of Regels Overheid Nederland (RONL), from
    sgort/linked-data-explorer; European Union Public Licence 1.2 (LICENSE
    in that folder). The twins of these models are under the EUPL-1.2 too,
    not under the Apache License.
  - dmn/bdt/: models of the Benefit Decision Toolkit, copyright (c) 2026
    Code for Philly; MIT licence (BDT_LICENSE in that folder).
  - dmn/clients/: Traffic Violation, from the examples of Apache KIE
    (incubating), Apache License 2.0; Dinner Decisions, from Camunda's
    examples, copyright 2013-2025 Camunda Services GmbH, Apache License 2.0.
  - epilog/: rule sheets and data sheets of the Epilog site
    (http://epilog.stanford.edu), Stanford University, copied in October
    2026 (epilog/README.md says which files were changed). EpilogJS, the
    engine of Epilog, is not included.
  - l4/: L4 examples, copyright 2025 Singapore Management University;
    Apache License 2.0 (LICENSE beside each). Some examples quote the laws
    they model (the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, United Kingdom Acts, New
    York regulations); those texts belong to their legislatures.
  - legalruleml/: examples of the LegalRuleML Core Specification Version
    1.0, copyright OASIS Open 2021, unmodified, under the notice of the
    specification (in full in legalruleml/NOTICE.txt). The twins beside
    them are not OASIS work products.
  - miniscript/: spending policies from Bitcoin Core (MIT licence,
    COPYING) and Liana (copyright the Liana developers, 3-clause BSD
    licence, LICENCE), whose files are kept unchanged in
    sources/originals/; and short quotations, with attribution, from
    Pieter Wuille's Miniscript page, which states no licence and whose
    authors keep all rights.
  - rulespec/: programs and tests of the Axiom Foundation's RuleSpec
    corpus (rulespec-us, https://github.com/TheAxiomFoundation/rulespec-us),
    unchanged; Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (NOTICE.txt
    in each sources/ folder). The twins beside them are translations of
    those programs and keep that attribution.
  - ruml/: examples of the RUML specification 1.0.0 and fixtures of its
    conformance tests (Law-as-Code-Institute/ruml), copyright 2026
    Rulemapping Group GmbH, published by Law as Code Institut GmbH; Apache
    License 2.0 (NOTICE.txt in that folder). RUML is a trademark of
    Rulemapping Group GmbH.
  - scasp/: programs from the tests of the s(CASP) package for SWI-Prolog
    (https://github.com/SWI-Prolog/sCASP), copyright 2019 Joaquin Arias,
    Apache License 2.0, copied unchanged. Thirteen are the s(CASP)
    translations that Logical English 1 made of its authors' examples; four
    are examples of s(CASP) and of s(ASP) (scasp/MAKING_OF.md).
  - spreadsheet/: the MLR 2025 Calculator and Formula Tool of the United
    States Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, a work of the United
    States Government (no copyright in the United States, 17 U.S.C. 105),
    copied unchanged (spreadsheet/README.md).
  The OIPA examples (oipa/) are not Oracle's: they were written for this
  repository, following the forms of Oracle's public OIPA XML Configuration
  Guide.
- examples/regulatory/ — models of public legal texts, which quote those
  texts for citation: United States federal texts (the Harmonized Tariff
  Schedule, Customs rulings, Medicare coverage documents, regulations and
  decisions), which are in the public domain; European Binding Tariff
  Information decisions, published by the European Commission for reuse; and
  published insurance filings and historical documents (underwriting/).
- examples/regulatory/bna.le — the British Nationality Act 1981 as a logic
  program, recovered from a 1980s micro-PROLOG program by M. J. Sergot,
  F. Sadri, R. A. Kowalski, F. Kriwaczek, P. Hammond and H. T. Cory ("The
  British Nationality Act as a logic program", Communications of the ACM,
  1986).
- examples/moreExamples/collections/kowalski-book/ — programs based on the
  examples of Robert Kowalski's book "Computational Logic and Human
  Thinking" (Cambridge University Press, 2011).

Names of other systems

